  "headline_blocker": {
    "issue": "The supplied VM's NVIDIA driver (550.144.03, CUDA 12.4) cannot execute a CUDA 13 runtime, so the pinned SGLang 0.5.20 and vLLM 0.30.0 stacks cannot run here.",
    "evidence_1": "SGLang 0.5.20 hard-pins torch==2.13.0; vLLM 0.30.0 hard-pins torch==2.13.0.",
    "evidence_2": "torch==2.13.0 on PyPI is a CUDA 13.0 build: requires cuda-toolkit[...]==13.0.3, nvidia-cudnn-cu13==9.20.0.48, nvidia-nccl-cu13, nvidia-nvshmem-cu13, cuda-bindings>=13.0.3.",
    "evidence_3": "torch 2.13.0 has no CUDA 12 build on any PyTorch index (cu126/cu128/cu129 newest is 2.9.1), so no CUDA 12 path exists for those pins.",
    "evidence_4": "Direct runtime probe: loading the CUDA 13 runtime reports cudaRuntimeGetVersion = 13.0 while cudaDriverGetVersion = 12.4, and cudaSetDevice(0) returns 35 (cudaErrorInsufficientDriver, 'CUDA driver version is insufficient for CUDA runtime version').",
    "evidence_5": "The VM is an unprivileged container, so the host driver cannot be upgraded, and the trellis-serve published Docker image ghcr.io/0xsero/sglang-exl3:v0.7.0-ampere cannot be used because Docker-in-Docker is unavailable.",
    "consequence": "Runtime B and Runtime C are recorded as INSTALL_FAILURE with the driver/CUDA-major mismatch as the cause. This is a property of the supplied environment, NOT a finding about EXL3 support in SGLang or vLLM, and NOT about MiniCPM5."
  },
